course: dictionary
lesson: 2595
title: Depart: leave, differ, or pass away
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Depart is a formal word for leave, especially when you start a journey. «The train departs at 9:40 and arrives at 11:05.» The clues are the time, the train and the journey, so depart means the train leaves at 9:40.
explain: Depart can also mean to do something different from what is usual or planned. The phrase is depart from. «The chef departed from the recipe and added chilli.» The clue is that the chef did not follow the usual plan, so depart from means to change the plan.
explain: Depart has a gentler, polite use too. «Departed this life» means died. It is old-fashioned and you will find it on old gravestones. «The dearly departed» means the dead, and «the departed» means the dead person or people. Polite words like these soften something sad.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in leave. «The train leaves at 9:40 and arrives at 11:05» still makes sense, so depart means leave. If the sentence is about a plan or a recipe, try do something different from instead. Always test your idea in the sentence.
fact: Depart is a formal word for leave, especially when starting a journey.
fact: «The train departs at 9:40 and arrives at 11:05» means the train leaves at 9:40 and reaches its stop at 11:05.
fact: Departure boards at stations and airports show when each train or plane departs.
fact: Depart from means to do something different from what is usual or planned.
fact: A euphemism is a softer, politer way of saying something hard: «the departed» means the dead.
exercise: «The bus departs at half past seven.» What does departs mean? | Breaks down | Arrives | *Leaves | Stops for lunch
exercise: The timetable said the train would leave at 8, but the driver departed from it. What does departed from mean here? | *Did something different from what was planned | Read the timetable aloud | Went back to the station | Left the train behind
exercise: On an old gravestone you read «Departed this life». What does it mean? | Found a lost dog | Moved house | *Died | Went on a holiday abroad
test: Which word could replace departs in «The plane departs at noon»? | Grows | Sings | Arrives | *Leaves
test: What does «the departed» mean? | People who are on holiday | *People who have died | People who have just arrived | People who are late for a train
exam: Which phrase politely means someone has died? | Went to the shops | Arrived early | Departed from the plan | *Departed this life
